  "strategy": "We'll use a combination of genetic algorithm and local optimization to beat the previous program's result. Our approach will be to:\n1. Generate multiple initial populations using different windowing functions (cosine, Gaussian, parabolic) and random noise\n2. Use tournament selection to pick the best individuals from each generation\n3. Apply crossover and mutation operations\n4. Periodically apply the LP method from the parent program to refine the best candidate\n\nDetailed steps:\n\n1. Approach and rationale:\n   - We'll use a genetic algorithm (GA) because it explores a broader search space than local optimization methods\n   - GA will generate diverse candidates through crossover and mutation\n   - LP local optimization will be applied periodically to refine promising candidates\n   - We'll use multiple initial populations (different window functions) to avoid local minima\n   - The search will focus on sequences of length 512 first, then try longer ones if time allows\n\n2. Mathematical specification (permitted, since not required):\n   - Objective function: minimize 2*n*max(convolve(a,a)) / sqrt(sum(a))^2\n   - Constraints: non-negative real numbers\n   - The sequence is represented as an array of n elements (a0, a1, ..., a_{n-1})\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   - Input parameters: seed (use for reproducibility), deadline (time when to stop)\n   - Steps:\n     1. Create initial population using windowing functions and random noise\n     2. For each candidate sequence in the population:\n        - Ensure sum is \u22650.01 (if not, multiply by 0.01/sum)\n        - Calculate objective using the provided formula\n     3. Initialize best_sequence to the first valid population member\n     4. Run a genetic algorithm loop until deadline is reached:\n        - Tournament selection (pick two candidates for crossover)\n        - Crossover operation (two-point crossover)\n        - Gaussian mutation (with rate controlled by standard deviation parameter)\n        - Apply LP optimization periodically\n        - Update best_sequence when a new better candidate is found\n     5. Final polish using LP method\n     6. Return the best_solution found\n\n4. Search choices and variations:\n   - Population configuration:\n        pop_size = 50\n        tournament_size = 5\n        mutation_prob = 0.1-0.3\n        crossover_type = two-point\n   - Hyperparameter adjustments:\n        mutation_strength = 0.05-0.3\n        elitism_rate = 0.1-0.2\n   - Cycle detection: stop if best_objective does not change for 200 iterations\n\n5. Budget and robustness estimation:\n   - Convolution operations for maximum: O(n^2) per evaluation (n=512 gives ~262144 operations)\n   - LP solves using simplex: O(k*n) per solve (~2e3 operations if n=2048)\n   - Total time: we'll use time estimates for the operations:\n        \u2022 Convolution: 0.1s for n=512\n        \u2022 LP solve: 0.5s for n=2048\n   - Total iterations: 1000\n   - Time bank: subtract overhead for 60s\n\n6. Validation and execution contract:\n   - For any new candidate:\n        \u2022 Clip to [0, max_val] (using next_max = max(0, min(1000, x)) for each x)\n        \u2022 Check sum and set to at least 0.01\n        \u2022 Recalculate score with final function\n   - If the candidate is better than current best, update best_sequence and best_score\n   - Print when: \n        \u2022 New best solution is found\n        \u2022 Deadline is approaching (every 10 generations)\n   - If execution exceeds deadline, return the longest saved solution with the best score\n   - Restart with different population if stuck",
